/**
# Class field defaults

A class field default is evaluated in one of three ways, chosen by the compiler
from the default expression:

- **Literal** (`x = 2`, `s = "hi"`, `ok = true`) — a scalar constant, stored
  once and shared by every instance. Cheap and immutable, so sharing is safe.
- **`(= expr)` lazy default** (`seq = (= n++)`) — a
  [ComputedExpr](../03_functions.gad) re-evaluated **per field, per instance**: each
  field with a `(=)` default is its own function call on construction.
- **Any other expression** (`y = g + 5`, `id = next()`, `items = [1, 2]`) — a
  per-instance default too, but all such fields of a class are computed together
  by a **single** `initFields` function call per construction (one call for many
  fields). This is why a composite default like `[1, 2]` is a fresh, independent
  value in every instance — never one shared object.

An explicitly passed field value always wins over its computed default.

The compiler lowers a class to a `Class("Name", (cls, define) => define(; …))`
call; a class with plain non-literal defaults gets an
`initFields = () => (; name = expr, …)` named argument (see classFieldsExpr).

Run it: `gad test samples/class`
**/

/**
A literal default is shared; a plain non-literal default (`nextId()`) is computed
fresh per instance — here an auto-incrementing id.
**/
test "per-instance non-literal default" {
    seq := 0
    nextId := func() { seq++; return seq }

    class User {
        id = nextId()   // per instance: 1, 2, 3, …
        name = "?"      // literal: shared default
    }

    t.equal(1, User().id)
    t.equal(2, User().id)
    t.equal(3, User().id)
    t.equal("?", User().name)
}

/**
A composite default (`[1, 2]`) is a fresh value per instance, so mutating one
instance's field never affects another's.
**/
test "fresh composite default per instance" {
    class Bag { items = [1, 2] }

    a := Bag()
    b := Bag()
    a.items[0] = 99

    t.equal(99, a.items[0])   // a was mutated
    t.equal(1, b.items[0])    // b is independent (not a shared array)
}

/**
The `(= expr)` form is a per-field lazy default: each `(=)` field is its own call
on construction. Useful for a per-instance counter or a fresh value. Postfix
`n++` yields the value **before** the increment; prefix `++n` the value after.
**/
test "(= expr) lazy default per field" {
    n := 0
    class Post { id = (= n++) }   // post-increment: old value
    t.equal(0, Post().id)
    t.equal(1, Post().id)
    t.equal(2, Post().id)

    m := 0
    class Pre { id = (= ++m) }    // pre-increment: new value
    t.equal(1, Pre().id)
    t.equal(2, Pre().id)
    t.equal(3, Pre().id)
}

/**
All three default kinds coexist: the literal is shared, the `(=)` field and the
plain non-literal are each per instance, and a passed value overrides its default.
**/
test "mixed defaults and override" {
    g := 100
    n := 0
    class C {
        x = 1           // literal
        y = g + 1       // plain non-literal -> initFields
        seq = (= n++)   // ComputedExpr -> per field
    }

    c1 := C()
    c2 := C()
    t.equal(1, c1.x)
    t.equal(101, c1.y)
    t.equal(0, c1.seq)   // post-increment: first instance gets 0
    t.equal(1, c2.seq)

    // A passed value wins over the computed default.
    t.equal(7, C(; y = 7).y)
}
